> What happens is that once the 120th *unique* black pudding is created,
> they are officially extinct. From that point on, they won't split any
> longer. This includes existing ones, i.e. those in a pudding farm.
>
> In normal games, you never hit this limit. You end up killing a dozen
> or so only. If you pudding-farm, you end up killing 255+, but most of
> those are offsprings of *one* unique pudding. If the showborn patch is
> installed, you'll get "255 black puddings (12 created)" or similiar in
> the dump log.
>
> This is quite easy to visualize on-screen. Find a pudding, #name it
> "Puddy" and split it with an iron object. Repeat this a few times and
> you will end up with a dozen puddings named "Puddy". Still, only *1*
> unique pudding was involved.
>
> In extinctionist games, where you often want to pudding-farm (to pray
> for spellbooks or to reduce anger etc.), you should #name all unique
> puddings with numbers (001, 002, ...). This allows you to distinguish
> new puddings that just got created from old ones which stepped into a
> teleporter trap or fell through a trapdoor. And once you #name "100",
> you better start to work that pudding farm, because your farming days
> will soon be over.
